11:30:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I need to push back here, because that specific claim isn't correct—and it's not a trivial distinction. **Earth is losing energy to space right now, and always has been** If it were true that added energy "stays here, period" with none leaking back to space, Earth's temperature would rise without limit, indefinitely, for as long as the sun kept shining—regardless of greenhouse gas levels. That's not what happens, and we can directly measure that it's not what happens. Satellites measure outgoing longwave radiation (infrared energy leaving Earth for space) continuously. It's a real, measured quantity—not a theoretical abstraction. Earth is radiating enormous amounts of energy into space every single second, right now, as you read this. That's simply an observed fact. **Why this actually matters for understanding global warming itself** Here's the important part: if energy didn't leak back to space at all, greenhouse gases wouldn't even need to be part of the explanation—any energy input at all would cause infinite runaway heating on a bare rock with no atmosphere too. That's clearly not what happens (the Moon doesn't heat up forever from sunlight). The actual mechanism of global warming *requires* that outgoing energy is reduced, not eliminated. Here's why the distinction matters: - Without greenhouse gases: Earth absorbs energy, radiates almost all of it back out efficiently, settles at a cooler equilibrium (roughly -18°C average, much colder than actual). - With greenhouse gases: Earth absorbs energy, but outgoing radiation is partially delayed/re-absorbed/re-emitted by the atmosphere, so *less* net energy escapes per unit time than would otherwise. This requires the surface to warm up further until enough energy is again escaping to balance what's coming in. - With *more* greenhouse gases: the outgoing bottleneck gets tighter, so an even higher temperature is needed to push enough energy back out to match input. Global warming isn't "no energy escapes"—it's "less energy escapes at any given temperature than before, so temperature has to rise to compensate." **The testable difference between these two ideas** If your version were correct (energy just accumulates, no leakage), we'd expect runaway, unbounded warming with no logical endpoint. If the mainstream version is correct (reduced but nonzero leakage, new equilibrium), we'd expect warming that rises and then levels off once a new balance point is reached, as long as CO2 concentrations stabilize. Historical climate data (like ice age cycles) show exactly this pattern—temperature rising, then plateauing at a new level, not spiraling indefinitely—which supports the equilibrium model, not the "zero energy loss" model. So on this specific point, I'd push back: it's not just semantics. The physical claim that no energy escapes back to space is testable, and it's false. What's true is that *less* escapes than would without greenhouse gases, which is sufficient to explain warming without requiring total containment.
